  "text": "Sure! Here's a Python function that meets your requirements:\n\n```python\nimport string\n\ndef word_histogram(text):\n    # Create a translation table to remove punctuation\n    translator = str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation)\n    \n    # Convert the text to lowercase and remove punctuation\n    cleaned_text = text.lower().translate(translator)\n    \n    # Split the text into words\n    words = cleaned_text.split()\n    \n    # Count frequency of each word\n    histogram = {}\n    for word in words:\n        histogram[word] = histogram.get(word, 0) + 1\n    \n    return histogram\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- **`str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation)`**: This creates a translation table that removes all punctuation.\n- **`.lower()`**: Converts the entire string to lowercase to ensure case-insensitive comparison.\n- **`.translate(translator)`**: Removes all punctuation from the string.\n- **`.split()`**: Splits the string into individual words.\n- **`histogram.get(word, 0) + 1`**: Uses the dictionary `get` method to count frequencies.\n\n### Example Usage:\n```python\nprint(word_histogram(\"Hello, world! Hello.\"))\n# Output: {'hello': 2, 'world': 1}\n\nprint(word_histogram(\"Python is great. Python is fun!\"))\n# Output: {'python': 2, 'is': 2, 'great': 1, 'fun': 1}\n\nprint(word_histogram(\"Test, test, TEST.\"))\n# Output: {'test': 3}\n```\n\nThis should pass all your unit tests! Let me know if you need a version that handles more complex cases (like apostrophes or hyphenated words).",
